{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Woe to him who says, Awake</b> — [this is] among the intransitive verbs, for [in] language one says, הקיצה משנתך ('awake from your sleep'), and likewise עורי, like עורה למה תישן. Therefore those who say 'the awakener of sleepers' have erred, for one who wakes sleepers should be said [as such]; והמקיץ רדומים is not correct, for the מקיץ is the sleeper himself. And the witness [is] that there is no seer and no knower and no מקיץ, for all of them are asleep, for a divinely sent slumber had fallen upon him [1 Samuel 26:12]. And it is written: לא הקיץ הנער — [the] mem [of] דומם is like the mem [of] ריקם and חנם, and the meaning is like ידמו כאבן. And according to Rabbi Judah's opinion: ידמו [is] of the doubled-root verbs, and likewise דומם, and it is an adjective." "<b>It teaches</b> that it is vapor, that it [the idol] is overlaid with gold and silver — the meaning concerns its limbs, for they are not like a man's limbs, which life binds together; therefore וכל רוח אין בקרבו, and the opposite of these."], :book :habakkuk, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Resolved ה' as divine abbreviation; 'divinely sent slumber' translates תרדמת השם where השם = HaShem."}, :source-witnesses [["<b>Ibn Ezra on Habakkuk</b>- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/havakuk"] ["Ibn Ezra on Habakkuk -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/havakuk"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:49:15.055293519Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}